      "question": [
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384344",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following are (virtually) shared by threads within a single process?<br>\nIn other words, is it instantiated per process instead of per thread? Select all that apply.</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>Heap</li>\n\t<li>Stack</li>\n\t<li>Code/Program Text</li>\n\t<li>Registers</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a;c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,goclasses,operating-system,process-and-threads,multiple-selects,one-mark",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384337",
          "text": "<p>If there are $\\mathrm{N}$ jobs, arrived at almost same time in the system, all of the same length(burst time) $\\text{L}.$ Round robin algorithm is used by the scheduler. Where $\\mathrm{T}$ is the length of the time slice, $\\mathrm{L}&gt;\\mathrm{T}$ and $\\mathrm{L}$ is multiple of $\\mathrm{T}$.<br>\nThen the first job will finish at approximately</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\text{L}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\mathrm{N}\\ast \\mathrm{~L}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\mathrm{N}\\ast \\mathrm{~L}-(\\mathrm{N}-1) \\ast \\mathrm{~T}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{N}\\ast \\mathrm{~L}-(\\mathrm{N}) \\ast \\mathrm{~T}$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,two-marks",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384351",
          "text": "<p>A starvation-free job-scheduling policy guarantees that no job waits indefinitely for service. Which of the following job-scheduling policies is starvation-free?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>Round-robin</li>\n\t<li>Priority queuing</li>\n\t<li>Shortest job first</li>\n\t<li>Youngest job first</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,one-mark",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384343",
          "text": "<p>Consider three processes $\\text{P1, P2,}$ and $\\text{P3}$ with respective arrival times of $0 \\mathrm{~ms}, 10 \\mathrm{~ms}$, and $20 \\mathrm{~ms}$ and respective processing times of $30 \\mathrm{~ms}, 15 \\mathrm{~ms}$, and $30 \\mathrm{~ms}$. The three processes are preemptively scheduled on a single-CPU system using the shortest-remaining-processing-time-first scheduling policy. Which of the following shows the order in which the processes complete, from first to last?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\mathrm{P}1 \\quad \\mathrm{P} 2 \\quad \\mathrm{P} 3$</li>\n\t<li>$\\mathrm{P}1 \\quad \\mathrm{P} 3 \\quad \\mathrm{P} 2$</li>\n\t<li>$\\mathrm{P} 2 \\quad \\mathrm{P} 1 \\quad \\mathrm{P} 3$</li>\n\t<li>$\\mathrm{P} 2 \\quad \\mathrm{P} 3 \\quad \\mathrm{P} 1$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,two-marks",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384353",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following statements are true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>A user-level process cannot modify its own page table entries.</li>\n\t<li>The scheduler is the part of an Operating System that determines the priority of each process.</li>\n\t<li>Shortest Remaining Time First is the best preemptive scheduling algorithm that can be implemented in an Operating System.</li>\n\t<li>No process will starve for CPU in round robin scheduling.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a;d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,multiple-selects,one-mark",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384339",
          "text": "An arriving thread is run at the beginning of its arrival time, if the scheduling policy allows it. Turnaround time is defined as the time a process takes to complete after it arrives.\n\nNow Consider the following data available for FIFO CPU Scheduling where we have $4$ processes $\\text{A, B, C, D.}$\n\n$$\\textbf{Entry Times}$$$$\\begin{array}{|c|c|}\n\\hline \\mathrm{A} & 1 \\\\\n\\hline \\mathrm{B} & 2 \\\\\n\\hline \\mathrm{C} & \\\\\n\\hline \\mathrm{D} & 8 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}\n\\hline \\downarrow \\textbf{Current Time} \\\\ \\qquad \\qquad \\qquad\n\\textbf{Scheduler} \\rightarrow &  \\textbf{FIFO} \\\\\n\\hline 1 & \\text{A} \\\\\n\\hline 2 & \\text{A} \\\\\n\\hline 3 & \\text{A} \\\\\n\\hline 4 & \\text{B} \\\\\n\\hline 5 & \\text{B} \\\\\n\\hline 6 & \\text{B} \\\\\n\\hline 7 & \\text{C} \\\\\n\\hline 8 & \\text{D} \\\\\n\\hline 9 & \\text{D} \\\\\n\\hline 10 & \\text{D} \\\\\n\\hline \\text{Avg Turnaround Time} & 3.5 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n$$\nWhat is the entry time for process $\\text{C}?$",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "5",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,numerical-answers,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,two-marks",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384331",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following processes with CPU burst time in ms. Assume all processes arrive at time $0.$<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}<br>\n\\hline \\textbf{Process} &amp; \\textbf{Burst Time (ms)} \\\\<br>\n\\hline \\text{P1} &amp; 10 \\\\<br>\n\\hline \\text{P2} &amp; 3 \\\\<br>\n\\hline \\text{P3} &amp; 8 \\\\<br>\n\\hline \\text{P4} &amp; 5 \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\end{array}<br>\n$$<br>\nIf the processes use First Come First Served Scheduling, for which of the following process order, the average waiting time of the processes will be minimum?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:decimal\">\n\t<li>$\\text{Order 1: P1 P2 P3 P4}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{Order 2: P2 P3 P4 P1}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{Order 3: P4 P3 P2 P1}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{Order 4: P4 P2 P3 P1}$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\text{Order 1}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{Order 2}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{Order 3}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{Order 4}$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,two-marks",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384349",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following transition diagram in which a thread moves between each of the $3$ states :</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p><p><br>Below we give some conditions that cause the thread to move between each of the $3$ states, and causes each arrow :</p><ul><li>$\\text{P} :$&nbsp; process is scheduled by the scheduler and executed by CPU.</li><li>$\\text{Q} :$ Time slice runs out, but the process is still wanting to run.</li><li>$\\text{R} :$ I/O completes, or lock is acquired. Woken up by a semaphore or conditional.</li><li>$\\text{S} :$ Any blocking action. I/O request, lock blocks.</li></ul><p>Match the transitions in the diagram with the conditions that cause them :<br>Which of the following is/are correct?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$\\text{1 – P}$</li><li>$\\text{2 – Q}$</li><li>$\\text{6 – R}$</li><li>$\\text{5 – S}$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a;b;d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,multiple-selects,one-mark",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384335",
          "text": "Consider the following Scheduling Algorithm \"Least Used First(LUF)\":\n\n$\\text{Least Used First (LUF):}$ When given the choice to schedule two processes on the run queue, the scheduler will select the one that has used the fewest CPU cycles thus far. In case of a tie, the queue is otherwise FIFO. When a process is descheduled it goes to the end of the queue.\n\n$\\text{Duration of a process:}$ The amount of CPU time it will need, in time units.\n\nConsider the following $4$ processes:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline \\textbf{Job} & \\textbf{Arrival Time} & \\textbf{Duration} \\\\\n\\hline \\text{A} & 0 & 25 \\\\\n\\hline \\text{B} & 15 & 25 \\\\\n\\hline \\text{C} & 25 & 5 \\\\\n\\hline \\text{D} & 40 & 5 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n$$\nSupposing a running job is only preempted when a new job arrives (no interrupts).\n\nAssume all jobs are purely CPU-bound.\n\nWhat is the average turn around time using LUF scheduling algorithm?",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "27.5",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,numerical-answers,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,two-marks",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384347",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following statements are true?:</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>The processor can be switched to kernel mode during execution of a user process, if the user process issues a system call.</li>\n\t<li>The processor can be switched to kernel mode during execution of a user process, without the user process issuing a system call.</li>\n\t<li>The number of threads in the system may exceed the number of CPU cores.</li>\n\t<li>To safely process system calls, the kernel MUST&nbsp;maintain a separate stack in the kernel for each thread in each user process.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "A;B;C",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,goclasses,operating-system,process-and-threads,multiple-selects,one-mark",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384333",
          "text": "Consider a system with a single CPU core and three processes $\\text{A, B, C}.$ Process $\\text{A}$ arrives at $t=0$, and runs on the CPU for $10$ time units before it finishes. Process $\\mathrm{B}$ arrives at $t=6$, and requires an initial $\\mathrm{CPU}$ time of $3$ units, after which it blocks to perform I/O for $3$ time units. After returning from I/O wait, it executes for a further $5$ units before terminating. Process $\\mathrm{C}$ arrives at $t=8$, and runs for $2$ units of time on the CPU before terminating. Recall that only the size of the current CPU burst (excluding the time spent for waiting on I/O ) is considered as the \"job size\" in these schedulers.\n\nFor Shortest Remaining Time First (preemptive) scheduling policy, what is the time of completion of process $\\text{C}?$",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "11",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,numerical-answers,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,two-marks",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384329",
          "text": "Consider the following set of jobs to be scheduled for execution on a single CPU system.\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|l|} \\hline\n\\textbf{Job} & \\textbf{Arrival Time} & \\textbf{Size(msec)} & \\textbf{Priority} \\\\\n\\hline \\text{J}_1 & 0 & 10 & 2 \\;\\text{(Silver)} \\\\\n\\text{J}_2 & 2 & 8 & 1 \\;\\text{(Gold)} \\\\\n\\text{J}_3 & 3 & 3 & 3 \\;\\text{(Bronze)} \\\\\n\\text{J}_4 & 10 & 4 & 2 \\;\\text{(Silver)} \\\\\n\\text{J}_5 & 12 & 1 & 3 \\;\\text{(Bronze)} \\\\\n\\text{J}_6 & 15 & 4 & 1 \\;\\text{(Gold)} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n$$\nThe completion time of process $\\text{J}_{5}$ using non-preemptive PRIORITY scheduling for these jobs is _________\n\nIn non-preemptive PRIORITY scheduling, jobs are executed based on their PRIORITY, and ties are broken in favor of the earliest arrival time.",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "30",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,numerical-answers,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,two-marks",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384342",
          "text": "<p>Four processes- $\\text{P}_1, \\text{P}_2, \\text{P}_3,$ and $\\text{P}_4-$ are scheduled to use a single processor. The following table shows the arrival time and duration of each of the four processes.<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}<br>\n\\hline \\textbf{Process} &amp; \\textbf{Arrival Time (seconds)} &amp; \\textbf{Duration (seconds)} \\\\<br>\n\\hline \\hline \\text{P}_1 &amp; 0 &amp; 7 \\\\<br>\n\\hline \\text{P}_2 &amp; 2 &amp; 4 \\\\<br>\n\\hline \\text{P}_3 &amp; 4 &amp; 1 \\\\<br>\n\\hline \\text{P}_4 &amp; 5 &amp; 4 \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\end{array}<br>\n$$<br>\nIf the system uses a preemptive shortest-job-first scheduling algorithm, what is the average waiting time for the four processes, in seconds?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$2$</li>\n\t<li>$3$</li>\n\t<li>$4$</li>\n\t<li>$5$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,two-marks",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384340",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following table showing a set of jobs to be processed on a single processor, the number of seconds of service that each requires, and each job's arrival time.<br>\n$$\\begin{array}{ccll}\\text { Job } &amp; \\begin{array}{c}\\text { Service Time } \\\\ \\text { (sec.) }\\end{array} &amp; \\text { Arrival time } \\\\\\hline 1 &amp; 2 &amp; t_0 \\\\ 2 &amp; 5 &amp; t_0 \\\\ 3 &amp; 1 &amp; t_0+1 \\;\\mathrm{sec} . \\\\ 4 &amp; 9 &nbsp;&amp; t_0+5\\; \\mathrm{sec} . \\\\ 5 &amp; 3 &amp; t_0+10 \\;\\mathrm{sec} . \\\\ 6 &amp; 2 &amp; t_0+15\\; \\mathrm{sec} .\\end{array}$$<br>\nIf a first-come-first-served scheduling algorithm is assumed, where ties are resolved in favor of the smaller numbered job, what is the average (arithmetic mean) number of seconds that a job waits before being assigned to the processor?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$20 / 6$</li>\n\t<li>$21 / 6$</li>\n\t<li>$23 / 6$</li>\n\t<li>$31 / 6$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,two-marks",
          "category": "Operating System"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "384341",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following table showing a set of jobs to be processed on a single processor, the number of seconds of service that each requires, and each job's arrival time.</p>\n\n<p>$$\\begin{array}{ccll}\\text { Job } &amp; \\begin{array}{c}\\text { Service Time } \\\\ \\text { (sec.) }\\end{array} &amp; \\text { Arrival time } \\\\\\hline 1 &amp; 2 &amp; t_0 \\\\ 2 &amp; 5 &amp; t_0 \\\\ 3 &amp; 1 &amp; t_0+1 \\;\\mathrm{sec} . \\\\ 4 &amp; 9 &nbsp;&amp; t_0+5\\; \\mathrm{sec} . \\\\ 5 &amp; 3 &amp; t_0+10 \\;\\mathrm{sec} . \\\\ 6 &amp; 2 &amp; t_0+15\\; \\mathrm{sec} .\\end{array}$$<br>\nIf a round-robin scheduling algorithm with a time slice of $10$ milliseconds is assumed, then at approximately what time is Job $3$ completed?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$t_0+22 \\; \\mathrm{sec}$.</li>\n\t<li>$t_0+8\\; \\mathrm{sec}$.</li>\n\t<li>$t_0+4\\; \\mathrm{sec}$.</li>\n\t<li>$t_0+2 \\;\\mathrm{sec}$.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-os-1,goclasses,operating-system,process-scheduling,two-marks",
          "category": "Operating System"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}